1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
On a gaming card with 16 GB of
VRAM, someone manages to run Qwen.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,840
A real 27-billion-parameter
model, at 101 tokens per second.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:13,480
Faster than you can read. Another user,
same card, same model: 13 tokens/s.

4
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:18,320
8 times less. And on my PC, this Qwen felt
really slow. So, is 16 GB enough or not?

5
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:23,440
Between 101 and 13, there's a big
difference. And that difference

6
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:27,380
is the file. One file that fits,
and one file that spills over.

7
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:31,540
So let's do your card's math. And at the
end, I'll tell you what was wrong on my side.

8
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:35,800
So, think of your card as a
budget. Three things share it:

9
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:40,320
the model file, the conversation memory,
and the agent, if you code with it.

10
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:45,320
The same model comes in several
files. Basically, it's the JPEG of AI.

11
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:49,700
You keep fewer bits per parameter, the file
gets lighter, and you lose a bit of quality.

12
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:54,680
That's called quantization. And here's the
first trap: the gigabytes. Your 16 GB card

13
00:00:54,680 --> 00:01:00,400
is actually 17.2 billion bytes.
Because memory is counted in packs

14
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:04,640
of 1024. The sites where you download
models, on the other hand, count in

15
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:09,040
round billions. So a 16.5
GB file, on paper, fits.

16
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:13,560
Except Windows also uses your card,
to display your screen for example.

17
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:19,860
And that takes about 1 GB. Result: the
Q4_K_M, the 4-bit file you're offered

18
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:28,320
most often, weighs between 16.5 GB and
19 GB, depending on who publishes it.

19
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,660
So it doesn't fit, before you've even
typed a word. There are tighter 4-bit

20
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:38,740
files, around 14 GB. And in 3 bits, a
well-made file is 12 GB, small acceleration

21
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:43,740
module included. That leaves you
about 4 GB, a bit less once the

22
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:50,060
engine has helped itself. And those 4 GB have
to be shared. Every token the model keeps

23
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:55,840
in mind takes up space on your card. And
for Qwen, we can calculate it: out of 64

24
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,320
layers, only 16 really keep the
conversation memory. So do the

25
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:07,820
multiplication: that's 64 kilobytes per
token. Doesn't sound like much. Except 8,000

26
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:14,860
tokens, LM Studio's default, is already
half a gig. And 128,000 is 8 GB.

27
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:20,860
More than everything that's left. With our 4
GB, you get about 60,000 tokens. And if you

28
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:25,980
switch that memory to 8 bits, an
engine setting, you get almost double.

29
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,500
And the third line is the one everyone
forgets: the agent. A coding agent

30
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,620
like Claude Code, before your first
question, sends its instructions and the

31
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:38,640
list of all its tools. One user counted
more than 50,000 tokens on the meter

32
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:44,640
before typing anything at all. On our
Qwen, that's more than 3 GB. Basically,

33
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:48,500
it's the game asking you for a 3 GB
patch before the title screen. So, if

34
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,820
you plug an agent into your card, here's
the math. With memory in the normal

35
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:59,540
version, you have barely 10,000 tokens
left for your real work. With memory in

36
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:05,820
8 bits, more than 70,000 tokens. It fits,
but only if you set up your card properly.

37
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:11,320
OK, now it fits. But how fast does
it go? To write a single token, the

38
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:16,340
card has to reread the whole model.
So the max speed is simple: the memory

39
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:22,300
speed divided by the file size. A
5080, for example, is 960 gigabytes per

40
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:27,300
second. With a 12 GB file, your
ceiling is around 80 tokens per

41
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:31,000
second. But in practice, you never
quite reach it. Someone with a

42
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,220
5060 Ti took a 14 GB 4-bit
file, exactly the one I had on

43
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:45,720
my PC. And they measure 25 tokens per
second, for a ceiling of 31. About 80%. Keep

44
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,800
that number in mind, you'll need it. But what
about the 101 tokens from the start? Without

45
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:56,500
tuning, the person who published them
measures 53. Then they turn on MTP, the small

46
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:00,580
acceleration module I mentioned.
The model guesses several words

47
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:06,020
ahead, and the card checks them all
at once. Result: 101. But that's a

48
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:11,120
single measurement. And now, the 13. When
the file doesn't fit, part of it goes

49
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:16,780
into your RAM. And RAM, even DDR5-6000
like mine, is about 96 gigabytes

50
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,440
per second. 10 times less than the graphics
card. And here, the rule is brutal:

51
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:25,060
the slowest memory sets the
pace. In a Spanish video, a

52
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:30,380
user loads a 4-bit model in LM
Studio, on a 5080. It doesn't fit.

53
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:36,240
They leave part of the layers in RAM,
and they say it themselves: 13 tokens per

54
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:40,120
second, because part of the model
is in RAM. It's not the same

55
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:44,040
settings as for the 101, so I'm not
comparing to the exact number. On a single

56
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:48,420
card, a 5060 Ti for example, someone
lived through both. With the previous

57
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:53,220
version of Qwen, their file spilled
over by 3 GB: 8 or 9 tokens per second.

58
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:58,740
With a file of the current Qwen that fits
on the card: 26. 3 times more. And their

59
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:03,040
conclusion, roughly: a file that fits
beats MTP. By the way, this also applies

60
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:07,740
to AMD. Look at memory
speed, not just the gigabytes

61
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:12,600
on the box. With the same file,
an RX 9070 XT has a ceiling twice

62
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:19,340
as high as an RX 9060 XT. Both have 16
GB. And the 5080 is higher still. And

63
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:23,340
if you don't have 16 GB, you're far
from alone. On Steam, in October, more

64
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:30,040
than 1 gamer in 4 has an 8 GB graphics
card, and 1 in 8 has 12. With 12, the 27B

65
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:35,700
fits in 3 bits, with a single 10
GB file. But you have barely 27,000

66
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:40,960
tokens left. A conversation, yes. A heavy
coding agent, no. With 8, it doesn't fit:

67
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:44,640
you end up in the 13 case, but worse. But
what about RAM in all this? For this model,

68
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:48,340
it's almost useless. Everything happens on
the card. Remember that, because with the next

69
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:52,640
model, it flips. Because there's
another Qwen, much bigger: the

70
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:57,700
Flash-Next. 125 billion parameters.
Actually, it's split into hundreds of

71
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:02,300
small specialists, experts. And for
each word, it only wakes up a few

72
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:06,780
of them: about 6 billion at work.
And an open-source project released

73
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:11,660
at the end of September, Strata, runs it
on a gaming PC. Its trick: the experts

74
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:16,400
used the most stay on your card,
the others go into RAM, and a big

75
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:21,540
table stays on the SSD. Except now,
everything flips. Strata's docs say it:

76
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:26,640
your RAM must hold the experts, plus
about 10 GB for Windows and the rest.

77
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:31,840
So do the math with me. The smallest
file of the full model is 34

78
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:39,140
GB of experts. Plus 10: 44. And I
have 32 GB of RAM. So, it's a no. But

79
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:44,160
the docs have an answer for 32 GB:
take the Coder model, a version

80
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:50,420
trimmed for code that keeps half the
experts. 23 GB plus 10: about 33.

81
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:56,820
And Windows lets you see about 33.5. So
it fits, just barely, with the browser

82
00:06:56,820 --> 00:07:01,440
closed. And that's a calculation. Except
one user measured it, with almost my

83
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:08,380
setup: the same processor, a 16 GB
5070 Ti, 31 GB of visible RAM. 70

84
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:13,920
tokens per second. A 125-billion
model, at that speed, on a gaming

85
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:17,620
PC. But at the top of their report,
the person writes it themselves: speed

86
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:24,000
only, no accuracy test. And there's
a price. Its authors claim 95% of

87
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,500
the full model's coding score, but
that's their number. On a developer

88
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:32,240
forum, someone feels, in essence,
a real loss. In a test published on

89
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:38,200
video, on a 5090, the Coder failed
to finish its task with Claude Code.

90
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:42,600
And according to the docs, it's weaker
outside English, Chinese, Japanese

91
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:47,380
and Korean. French is one of those. And if
you're thinking: well, I'll just add more RAM?

92
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:52,680
But I bought those 32 GB for 93
euros. Today, the same kit costs

93
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:58,960
560 euros. That's 6 times the price, for
the same sticks. Ouch. Tough luck for

94
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:03,100
Guillaume. Anyway, on paper,
the Coder fits on my PC. So next

95
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:08,660
week, I'm running a real test on my
5080, and we'll see what it's worth.

96
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:12,580
But let's say it runs on your PC. Is
it worth your subscription? On the

97
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:19,740
chart published by Qwen, the 27B beats
the old Opus 4.6 at code on one test, and

98
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:23,960
loses on another. But that's their
chart, with the full model, not

99
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:28,360
the 3-bit file that fits on your card.
An independent ranking, though, compares

100
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:36,360
the same 27B to Claude Opus 5, as an agent
in the terminal: 6% success versus 46. Oh my

101
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:43,400
god! Yeah, you heard that right: 6. So
for agentic coding on a real project,

102
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:48,000
Claude is way ahead. So, here's
how I see it. Your 16 GB model

103
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,260
is a very good plan B: your data
stays with you, it works without

104
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:55,260
internet, and you don't need to pay
every month. For serious agentic

105
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:59,940
coding, the subscription still has the edge.
And on top of that, with Claude, the 3 GB

106
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:04,480
patch isn't paid for by your card.
And my Qwen that felt so slow,

107
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:13,540
then? I had taken the 14 GB 4-bit
file. Remember: the 5060 Ti gets 25

108
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:19,800
tokens per second out of it, and my card
has twice the bandwidth. According to the

109
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:25,560
math, mine should have run at around
50. So it wasn't the card. That leaves

110
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:30,940
2 suspects. The first one, I'm sure it was
there: by default, this Qwen thinks at the

111
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:36,800
maximum level before answering. One
developer counted more than 22,000 tokens

112
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:43,820
of reasoning for a single request:
drawing a pelican. Even at 50 tokens per

113
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:49,920
second, that's more than 7 minutes before
producing the first useful line. That

114
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:53,680
reasoning level, you can lower it in
the right-hand panel of LM Studio. The

115
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:58,720
second suspect is me. With a 14 GB
file, once the screen was served, I had

116
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:04,960
less than 2 GB left for the conversation.
And I had pushed the conversation to 64,000

117
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:11,980
tokens, then to 100,000. If we go back
to line 2 of the bill: 64,000 tokens is

118
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:19,760
more than 4 GB. 100,000, more than 6.
So I was short by 2 to 4.5 GB. And when

119
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:23,960
your card is full, the NVIDIA driver
silently spills over into RAM instead of

120
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:28,480
crashing. It works, but much slower. NVIDIA
says so itself, and so does LM Studio.

121
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:34,380
Basically, according to the math, I had recreated
the 13 from the start, all by myself. To

122
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:38,160
find out, just open your Task Manager
and look at the performance of the

123
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:43,080
GPU. And watch the shared GPU memory
while the model is writing. If

124
00:10:43,080 --> 00:10:47,060
it climbs, you've found your culprit. And in
the NVIDIA control panel, a setting blocks this

125
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:52,000
spillover: the model crashes instead of
crawling, but at least you know. Anyway, my

126
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,760
card wasn't the problem: it was what I
was asking of it. So yes, 16 GB is enough

127
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:01,400
for a real 27-billion-parameter
model, if you pick the file that

128
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:06,080
fits, and if you leave room for the
conversation and the agent. To go

129
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:11,220
bigger, it's your RAM that decides,
not the card. And for agentic coding,

130
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:15,740
Claude stays ahead. For now, I'm keeping
my setup: the engines keep getting

131
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:19,300
better. But I'm going to switch
from LM Studio to engines like

132
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:23,940
llama.cpp, or to Strata. If you've never
installed an AI at home, I have a video

133
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:28,380
that walks you through it, I'll put
it in a card. And next week, we'll see

134
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:33,020
Strata for real on my card. In the meantime, tell
me your graphics card and RAM in the comments,

135
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:38,440
and I'll tell you what
fits on your PC. See you!

